**Read:** [Principles of Extractive Metallurgy — Tarun Kumar Kundu, NPTEL](../sources/Principles-of-Extractive-Metallurgy-NPTEL.lingua#r_7VCNEHN5VCQDDQTZ512PSHFZ78), all Faraday/cell reduction and refining examples; [Non-ferrous Extractive Metallurgy — Ray and Pugazhenthy, NPTEL](../sources/Nonferrous-Extractive-Metallurgy-NPTEL.lingua#r_29G1ZF58Y8EG123MNX3ZRNRE2W), the complete applicable electrometallurgical cases; [Non-ferrous Metals Industries — European Commission BREF](../sources/Nonferrous-Metals-Industries-BREF.lingua#r_19ZD7KKDWVWM20MXVNCQZJMCDB), one whole industrial cell/product/energy case.

For a defined deposition reaction, theoretical deposited mass is ItM/(nF) under the stated current/time and reaction basis. A current efficiency η attributes only a fraction of the total charge to that metal reaction. Useful recovery and accepted product yield are additional questions involving dissolution, shorting, morphology, detachment, contamination, handling, and other losses.

For invented copper deposition at 1000 A for one hour, η = 0.95, M = 63.546 g mol⁻¹, and n = 2, calculate ηItM/(2F); it is about 1.13 kg. Independently check the charge and unit conversions. An input voltage is needed for electrical energy; heat and upstream solution preparation require a wider account.

Define how η was measured and which mass, time, composition, and inventory observations support it. A high cathode mass can include retained liquid or impurities, and product removed during a period may have been deposited earlier. Reconcile measurement timing and support before interpreting a performance change.

**Work:** reconstruct a full deposition/refining campaign with charge, mass, assay, moisture, inventory, and voltage evidence. Independently calculate theoretical and useful output, uncertainty, and energy per accepted product. Compare a plausible current-efficiency change with a sampling or inventory explanation. Explain why a favorable charge balance alone does not validate purity, morphology, or whole-process recovery.

